          "text": "Confirmed target names: `common_cpp_static`, `common_c_static`, `string_utils_cpp_static`, `string_utils_c_static`, and shared `common_cpp_shared`, `common_c_shared`. So the settings regex covers shared DLLs. Good.\n\nImportant: unit tests link `string_utils_*_static` and `common_*_static` → those are static, inside test exes. So the same source can be instrumented in:\n- unit test exe (static copy)\n- t1/t2 main.exe (static copy)\n- t3/t4 main.exe + common_*_shared.dll (shared copy)\n\nWithin one coverage session all are loaded → same lines appear in multiple modules. That's the union question. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, ВЫСОКИЙ that root will double count across modules]\n\nLet me verify with existing .coverage? I can't merge (write). But actually I could inspect the existing separate XMLs: t1_c e2e vs unit. They're separate sessions. Can't conclude duplication across a single session. Fine.\n\nNow, one more check for the plan: does the coverage build already exist and is it configured with the latest CMakeLists? `build/coverage` exists. After we edit coverage.cmake (script only), no reconfigure needed. But we might need to re-run `cmake --preset coverage` if the user changed CMakeLists since. The last commit is after coverage work. I'll include `cmake --preset coverage` as a cheap step (no --fresh to save 30 s... but stale cache could hide problems; the journal says they used --fresh often. For correctness of the \"one command\" claim, I'd run `--fresh` once).\n\nHmm, `cmake --fresh` deletes CMakeCache and CMakeFiles, then configures. With vcpkg that re-detects triplet (30 s on Windows first time, then cached). Fine.\n\nNow about \"one command\" policy (§ 4.9 п.1): \"с чистого клона до зелёных тестов — одна команда\". Coverage has its own: `cmake --preset coverage && cmake --build build/coverage --target coverage`. Two commands; can be one target? `cmake --build` requires configure first. Acceptable — it's a separate mode.\n\nNow, let me think about whether to add the coverage target to `README` \"как прогнать\" — yes, Phase 6.\n\nNow, potential blocker: the full run includes `t4` e2e with `t4_radius_inner_quote` etc. that rely on boost process quoting; already tested in Debug. Fine.\n\nPotential blocker: the coverage run will run unit tests that in Coverage config have no ASan — fine.\n\nPotential blocker: 591 tests; ctest sequential; per-process instrumentation startup ~0.1–0.5 s → maybe 3–5 minutes. Acceptable.\n\nPotential blocker: dynamic instrumentation requires the process to be launched by the collector; grandchildren via boost::process are collected (verified).\n\nNow let me think about the negative control for \"report then fail\":\n- Break one unit test expectation (e.g., parse_int32 OkCase 5→6) → `cmake --build build/coverage --target coverage` should: build ok, tests fail, report produced, target exits non-zero.\n- Check the XML exists and contains data after failure.\n- Revert and confirm green.\n\nThat's a strong check of the new failure semantics.\n\nAnother verification: that `coverage` target actually rebuilds product targets (DEPENDS all) — `cmake --build build/coverage --target coverage` on a clean build dir would prove it, but a `--clean-first` + coverage run is expensive (~5-10 min). Maybe verify `ninja -t query coverage`? Simpler: after a full run, touch a source file and run coverage again; observe that the target re-runs tests. Hmm, that's another 3 min. Could check `ninja -C build/coverage -n coverage` to see the dependency expansion (read-only). Good: `ninja -t query coverage` shows deps? For custom target with DEPENDS all, the phony target `all` is a dependency → query shows it. That's a cheap check.\n\nNow, \"high-value\" verification that coverage reflects behavior: compare t1-only (41%) vs full run (should be >41%). Natural.\n\nNow let me think about what \"разбор непокрытых веток\" deliverable looks like concretely. After the full run:\n- produce a table per file: %, uncovered line numbers.\n- Go through top uncovered spots and classify: (a) test gap → add test; (b) unreachable (e.g., `*start != '\\0'` in parse_int32 — the journal already identified it as unreachable → candidate for deletion); (c) platform-specific or defensive.\n- That's real analysis work, and it's the actual course deliverable. It might exceed one session; I'll scope: this session = full run + first pass at gaps in `common/` (since those are shared by all tasks), and leave t*/main.c gaps for next.\n\nHmm, but the user's chosen option was \"Полный прогон coverage по t1–t4 ... получить полный XML и разобрать непокрытые места\". So yes, analysis included.\n\nNow, a key question: should we also *fix* the obvious unreachable line `*start != '\\0'` in parse_int32 (found in the journal's manual exercise)? Deleting dead code is a real improvement, and coverage would flag it. But careful: the journal noted it as \"кандидат на удаление\" — not decided. If we delete it, tests must stay green. I'll include it as an optional step in the analysis phase, contingent on the coverage report confirming it's never hit in any module (currently 0 hits in t1; after full run check again).\n\nWait — is it truly unreachable? `while (isspace((unsigned char)*start) && *start != '\\0')` — if `*start == '\\0'`, `isspace('\\0')` is false, so the second operand is never evaluated... but that's about *evaluating* the condition, not about the line being \"unreachable\". Line coverage: the line is executed every loop iteration (the condition is on the line). Coverage tools report line executed if the whole expression is evaluated. So it will show hits>0, not uncovered! The journal's claim \"ветка `*start != '\\0'` никогда не достигается\" is about the *branch*, not the line. With no branch coverage, we can't prove it. Hmm — careful. That's a good subtlety to raise: dead *branch* vs dead *line*. The line has hits; only branch data would show that `*start != '\\0'`'s false-exit is never taken. So this is exactly