          "text": "The user asks two clarifying questions. I need to answer precisely, with facts, and correct/refine.\n\nQ1: What happens now if a test under coverage fails — does it not get coverage? Where is this behavior specified?\n\nLet me be precise. Behavior comes from `cmake/coverage.cmake` lines 18-35:\n\n```\nexecute_process(COMMAND collect ... RESULT_VARIABLE _collect_rc)\nif(NOT _collect_rc EQUAL 0)\n  message(FATAL_ERROR \"coverage collection failed (exit code ${_collect_rc})\")\nendif()\nif(NOT EXISTS \"${_raw}\") ...\nexecute_process(merge ...)\n```\n\nSo: `collect` runs ctest as a child. If any test fails, ctest returns non-zero (in this case 8 or the number of failed tests). `collect` propagates that exit code (verified earlier in the journal with the no-tests experiment: collect exit: 8). So `_collect_rc != 0` → `FATAL_ERROR` before merge. That means:\n- The raw `.coverage` file **is** created by collect (usually — depends on whether collect writes output before propagating error). Actually in the journal experiment with `__no_such_test__` and `--no-tests=error`, the output said \"Результаты по объемам протестированного кода: build/coverage/probe/no_tests.coverage\" — so collect DID write the coverage output even though ctest failed. And `collect exit: 8`.\n- So the raw data exists, but `coverage.cmake` aborts before `merge` → no Cobertura XML, and no summary. The build target fails.\n\nSo the answer: coverage for failing tests is *collected* by the tool into the .coverage file, but your wrapper throws it away before converting. It's not a property of the tool — it's your script's early return.\n\nBut wait — is it true that collect writes the .coverage file even on test failure? In the journal, yes for the no-tests case (file existed). [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ for that case]. Whether it writes on a genuine test failure vs no-tests — likely same mechanism but not verified. Should say: verified for the no-tests case; for ordinary failed test — [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, ВЫСОКИЙ] but must be verified in step 5.1.\n\nAlso nuance: does `collect` return the child's exit code exactly? Journal: ctest exit 8 → collect exit 8. [ФАКТ] for that case.\n\nAlso, `data collector` behavior: it collects data for the whole session; if the child process crashes, data may be incomplete for that process but other processes' data remains.\n\nWhere is it \"прописано\": `cmake/coverage.cmake:30-32` — your own code. Not in the tool docs. Also `-t/--timeout` exists but not used.\n\nQ2: Aggregation — did I say aggregation exists? Let me be precise and honest:\n\n- The tool aggregates per-module/per-class and produces the root `<coverage line-rate>` = sum over classes. Verified: sum = root.\n- The split into two `<class>` entries for one file is by **anonymous namespace**, not by module. So the \"same file appears twice\" is not duplication of lines.\n- But: this is *not* a configuration in `coverage.settings.xml`. The settings only select which modules/sources are included/excluded. Grouping into `<class>` is the tool's own output format. Nothing in the settings controls that.\n- The settings do NOT deduplicate. They filter.\n\nSo what is \"deduplication\" I meant? Define precisely:\n\nThere are three distinct things:\n1. **Grouping** — one source file may produce several `<class>` records (anonymous namespace, and possibly several modules). The tool's root number sums *all* of them. That's fine as long as the same (file, line) doesn't appear in two records *from different modules that were both executed*.\n2. **Double counting** — if the same source is compiled into two binaries both loaded in the session (e.g., `common_cpp_static` inside unit test exe and `common_cpp_shared.dll` inside t3), then the same (file, line) appears twice as \"valid\", and hits are counted in both copies. The root rate is then a weighted average over *copies*, not over source lines. Whether that's \"wrong\" depends on what you want to measure: per-module coverage (fine) vs per-source coverage (needs dedup by (file,line) with OR of hits).\n3. **Deduplication** = collapse all records by (normalized file path, line number): valid = union of line numbers, covered = union of lines with hits>0 in at least one record. Then compute per-file and overall. That's what a summary tool would do.\n\nAnd what is currently configured in settings: only module/source filtering + CollectFromChildProcesses + instrumentation kind. No dedup.\n\nI should also correct the earlier statement that \"root percent is correct\" — it's correct *as a per-copy aggregate*. For t1 there is only one copy of each file loaded, so root = per-source. For the full run, if static+shared both appear, root ≠ per-source. That's the distinction.\n\nAlso: does the full run actually load both copies? t1/t2 main.exe link static; unit tests link static; t3/t4 link shared. So yes, in one session: `common_cpp_static` code (inside unit test exes + t1/t2 main.exe) and `common_cpp_shared.dll` (t3/t4). Both loaded → both instrumented → the same source lines appear in two modules. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ that both are linked; ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ that both appear in report — verify in step 5.3]\n\nAlso note: `string_utils_cpp_static` vs `common_cpp_static` — both contain string_utils.cpp. Unit tests for parse_int32 link `string_utils_cpp_static`, while parse_point links `common_cpp_static`. So even within unit tests there are two static copies of string_utils.cpp! Both may be loaded in different processes. That's another source of multiple records. Good detail.\n\nAlso in the t1_cpp report, there were only 4 files, each appearing 1-2 times (2 for string_utils due to anon namespace). So for t1 only one module copy was loaded. Confirms: per-run, only loaded modules appear.\n\nNow, should I mention that the setting `IncludeTestAssembly=True` doesn't do dedup? Yes, maybe briefly: it controls whether test assemblies themselves are included, not merging.\n\nLet me also double-check whether the tool's XML groups by module at all — maybe the `<class>` entries are per module+namespace, and the `name` attribute distinguishes. In t1_cpp, class #2 name='' and class #1 name='static unsigned __int64 `anonymous namespace`'. Hmm, that's odd: class name is the *namespace name*? For C++ coverage, the tool emits `<class name=\"<namespace>\">`. So all functions in a namespace group into a class. For C, there's no namespace → one class per module? Possibly.\n\nIf that's true, then in a full run, the same file compiled in static and shared would produce separate class entries both with name='' — indistinguishable except by their line sets/module. The Cobertura format from this tool may not include a module attribute. That's important for dedup: you can't tell from the XML *which module* a class came from — only the filename. So dedup can only be by filename+line union. Which is exactly what's needed for per-source.\n\nHmm, but then you also can't detect *which* copy. Fine.\n\nLet me verify whether `<class>` has any module info in the existing XML — check attributes and packages. Let me inspect the raw XML around a class for t1_c and see `<package name=\"main\">`. The package name was \"main\". So packages group by... module name? `package name=\"main\"` — maybe the module. For t1_c: only main.exe. For t1_cpp: package main too. So package = module name (main.exe). For the full run there'd be packages \"main\" (multiple exes?) and \"common_cpp_shared\" etc. That could give module info! Let me verify by inspecting the XML structure of unit.cobertura.xml (module = parse_int32_cpp.exe).\n\nLet me check.\n\nThis matters for the dedup explanation: if packages carry module names, you can distinguish copies.\n\nLet me run an inspection.\n\nAlso for Q1, I want to show the exact lines and maybe verify what collect does on a failed test? I can't run (that would... running collect writes files — forbidden in plan mode). So I'll cite the journal's no-tests experiment as evidence and mark ordinary failure as assumption.\n\nLet me inspect the XML packages.",
