          "text": "Excellent: `<package name=\"...\">` = **module name** (e.g., `parse_int32_c`, `main`). So module info IS present in Cobertura output — via package name. That means dedup can distinguish copies and can report per-module too.\n\nNote: for the exe targets, the output name is `main` (OUTPUT_NAME main), so package name = \"main\" for all 8 task exes — they'd collide! Hmm: all task exes have OUTPUT_NAME main, so their package name would be \"main\" for each. In a single report there would be multiple `<package name=\"main\">` entries (one per module)? Likely yes — the report is a list, duplicates by name possible. For t1_c only one. For full run, many \"main\" packages.\n\nHmm wait: package name might be the module's assembly name... For unit test `parse_int32_c` the package name matches the exe name. For t1_c the exe file is `main.exe` → package \"main\". So in a full run: 8 packages named \"main\" (t1..t4 × c/cpp), plus `common_c_shared`/`common_cpp_shared` packages, plus unit test exes. So package name is not a unique module id, but combined with the class filenames it's still enough? Not for dedup — dedup only needs filename+line.\n\nBut for reporting \"per module\" you'd need to know which main.exe — the XML doesn't say. So per-module reporting is ambiguous for tasks (all named \"main\"). Interesting detail but not critical: per-source is what matters.\n\nNow, does the report include *both* the module's own package and its dependencies? For t1_c: package \"main\" contained common files. Because common is statically linked into main.exe → same module. For t3 (shared), there'd be package \"main\" (main.c) and package \"common_c_shared\" (common files). Good.\n\nNow let me answer Q1 and Q2 precisely.\n\nQ1 details:\n- Behavior is defined in **your** `cmake/coverage.cmake:18-35`, not by the tool. Sequence: execute_process(collect) → if rc != 0 → FATAL_ERROR → merge never runs.\n- The tool itself: collect runs ctest as child; when tests fail, ctest's exit code propagates (verified earlier: `collect exit: 8` in the no-tests experiment, journal 2026-10-26). The raw `.coverage` file **was created** in that experiment despite non-zero exit. So coverage data for failing runs is not necessarily lost by the tool; your script discards it.\n- Nuance: data for a *crashed* process may be incomplete/absent, but data for other tests remains. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n- So \"если тест падает — на него не выдаётся покрытие\" is **not accurate**: data is collected and written; the wrapper refuses to convert it.\n- The exact verification for an ordinary failing test is planned as step 5.1 (I haven't run it).\n\nQ2:\n- \"Агрегация есть\" — clarify: the tool *sums* line records into the root line-rate. That's not configurable aggregation; it's output. Verified sum = root.\n- The two `<class>` entries are per-namespace grouping, not duplicates.\n- `coverage.settings.xml` does **filtering** (ModulePaths/Sources Include/Exclude) + CollectFromChildProcesses + instrumentation mode. It does **not** aggregate or dedup. Nothing there controls grouping.\n- Module name IS present in Cobertura: `<package name=\"...\">` = module (`main`, `parse_int32_c`). So the report distinguishes modules at package level.\n- \"Дедупликация\" = collapse records by (file, line): valid = union of line numbers across all records whose source file is the same; covered = union of lines with hits>0 in at least one record. Needed when the *same source* is compiled into several loaded modules (static copy in unit-test exe + shared DLL in t3/t4), because then root = average over copies, not over source lines.\n- Concrete: parse_int32_c unit exe links `string_utils_c_static`; parse_point_c links `common_c_static`; t1_c/t2_c main.exe link static; t3/t4 link `common_c_shared.dll`. In one session, string_utils.c exists in at least: parse_int32_c.exe, t1 main.exe, t2 main.exe, common_c_shared.dll, plus others. Root will weigh each copy.\n- What this means practically: root