          "text": "The user points out an inconsistency: branch vs block. Answer briefly and precisely.\n\nDefinitions:\n- Block (basic block) — a linear sequence of instructions with no branching inside; branches at the edges. Block coverage = was this block executed.\n- Branch — edge/outcome of a decision (true/false of an if, each case). Branch coverage = which outcomes were taken. Requires block-level structure/edges.\n- Line coverage = projection of block hits onto lines.\n\nThey are related but not the same. Branch coverage needs block/edge data; block coverage alone (which blocks executed) doesn't fully give branch coverage (you need the edges/transitions), but from block execution sets you can often infer untaken edges if you know the CFG — the tool knows the CFG, so if it reports blocks/edges, branch is derivable.\n\nCobertura's `branch-rate` in this output is nominal (=1, branch=\"False\") — i.e., Cobertura here carries no branch info at all. Whether the native xml has blocks/edges is what we check. If it has blocks but no edges, it's still enough to reason about individual conditions sometimes, but not a clean branch metric.\n\nSo: \"Если блочных данных нет\" — I meant \"нет данных о блоках/рёбрах (из которых выводится branch)\". Better phrasing: check for block/edge data; branch is the derived metric.\n\nBe brief: 1 short paragraph + a 3-line table maybe.\n\nAlso clarify: `branch=\"False\"` per line means \"this line is not a branch point\" — actually in Cobertura, `branch` attribute on `<line>` indicates whether the line has branch info. Here it's always False, and branch-rate=1 means \"no branches recorded\", not \"all branches covered\". That's the \"nominal\" issue: a tool/report consumer may read 100%.\n\nKeep it short.",
