          "text": "Now check for errors/bugs.\n\n1. `file(REMOVE ...)` at lines 12-13 removes `coverage.coverage` and `coverage.cobertura.xml`, but **does not remove `coverage.xml`**. So a stale native xml from a previous run survives if the new merge fails. Since merge failure triggers FATAL_ERROR after... wait, if `merge -f xml` fails, `_xml` may be stale (from previous run) — but they check `_merge_xml` rc and fail; however `if(NOT EXISTS \"${_xml}\")` would pass because stale file exists, so no error — but we already failed on rc. The real issue: on `collect` failure or merge failure, a stale coverage.xml remains, and a human might read the old report thinking it's new. Same for the general principle: all artifacts should be removed at start. **Bug (minor but real).** Fix: add `\"${REPORT_DIR}/coverage.xml\"` to `file(REMOVE ...)`.\n\n2. Variable reuse: `_line_rate` used for both cobertura line-rate and block coverage. It works because `string(REGEX MATCH)` overwrites and `CMAKE_MATCH_1` is updated. But `string(REGEX MATCH)` when no match sets CMAKE_MATCH_1 to empty and sets the output var to empty. So `if(CMAKE_MATCH_1)` works. However: naming a block metric `_line_rate` is a bug-in-waiting (misleading), and if the second regex doesn't match, `_line_rate` becomes empty — harmless. Recommend rename `_block_coverage`. [Minor]\n\n3. `string(REGEX MATCH \"line-rate=\\\"([0-9.]+)\\\"\" ...)` — first match in the file. For Cobertura, the first occurrence is the root `<coverage line-rate=...>`. We verified. But fragile if attribute order changes — acceptable. However there's a subtlety: `string(REGEX MATCH)` returns the whole match, not the group; they use CMAKE_MATCH_1 — correct.\n\n4. `block_coverage` regex takes the **first** occurrence in native xml. The first `<module>` in the report — is it the first module in document order. Which module is first? For a multi-module report, the first module may be `main.exe` of t1 or some other. So \"block coverage\" printed would be **one module's** block coverage, not overall. That's a real issue: the number looks like an overall metric but is per-module. In native xml there's no root aggregate; `<results>` has no attributes. So printing \"block coverage: 42.62\" is ambiguous/misleading. Need either: aggregate over product modules, or label it with the module name (e.g., `block coverage (main.exe): 42.62`), or print per-module list.\n\nThat's the most important finding: **the metric is per-module, not overall**, and in the full run there will be many modules named main.exe + common_*_shared.dll. Printing the first one will be arbitrary (order may vary between runs!). That's a real defect for a full run: the printed number may be one arbitrary module.\n\n5. `-f xml` merge writes `coverage.xml`; the `file(READ)` of the native xml is fine.\n\n6. Missing: the `collect` failure semantics (report-then-fail) is still not implemented — that was a decision from the previous turn, but the user asked only to check their change. I should note it's still pending (lines 32-34). Also note the `_collect_rc` early fail means if a test fails, no merge at all — and now both reports missing. This is the agreed change not yet done.\n\n7. Trailing whitespace at line 59 `endif() ` — clang-format doesn't cover .cmake; but `.editorconfig` maybe trims. Minor style. Actually `format-check` only handles C/C++ sources. Not a functional error; mention as nit.\n\n8. `file(REMOVE)` also should remove the `.xml` — point 1.\n\n9. Another potential issue: two merges both parse the same raw file; the second may be slower but fine.\n\n10. `if(NOT _merge_xml EQUAL 0)` — variable name `_merge_xml` is also the path? No, `_xml` is the path, `_merge_xml` is the rc. Confusing naming but works.\n\n11. Verify CMake semantics: `execute_process` RESULT_VARIABLE returns exit code as string; `if(NOT _merge_xml EQUAL 0)` — numeric compare works for \"0\"/\"1\". If process fails to launch (e.g., not found), RESULT_VARIABLE gets an error