          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks a conceptual question: why do \"red tests\" and \"inability to instrument\" merge? And why is it OK to produce a report if instrumentation failed (nonzero code) — isn't the report produced BY instrumentation?\n\nAnswer briefly and precisely.\n\nKey concepts:\n1. `collect` returns one exit code. It's a wrapper: it launches the collector session, spawns ctest as a child, waits, then writes the .coverage and exits. Its exit code reflects \"the overall operation\" — we observed it equals the child's code when tests fail. But if the collector itself has a problem, it can also return non-zero. There's no separate channel for \"which of the two\". So both look the same to us: non-zero rc. That's the \"merge\".\n\n2. But the user's second question: if instrumentation failed, why produce a report at all? Clarify: there are degrees of failure.\n   - \"Collection failed\" may mean partial failure: some processes were instrumented, some weren't (e.g., access denied on one process, skipped module, one test crashed). In that case the .coverage still contains data for the processes that worked — a partial but real report. Report is still meaningful.\n   - Total failure (tool didn't start, no session) → .coverage is not created → we catch it at the EXISTS check and don't produce anything.\n   So: report is produced from the raw data that exists; if raw exists, there is something to merge. The report is not \"produced by instrumentation happening successfully\" — it's a conversion of whatever data was collected.\n\n3. Also: the .coverage file is written at the END of the session (when the collector shuts down), aggregating all hits. So even with failing tests, the data up to that point is there.\n\n4. Important nuance: if instrumentation failed for a process, that process contributes no data; coverage will show those lines as uncovered — which is exactly why the report must be inspected with the knowledge that the run was not clean. That's the risk: a report from a partially-failed run can *look* like \"uncovered code\". So the value is diagnostic (see what ran), but the numbers are not comparable to a clean run.\n\n5. That's also why the message should not claim \"tests failed\" and why the run must be non-green.\n\nAlso clarify: the .coverage is binary; merge is just format conversion, no re-instrumentation. So \"отчёт формируется не от самой инструментации\" — well, data comes from instrumentation, but the merge step doesn't need instrumentation to work; it needs the data file.\n\nShort answer structure:\n- collect = one process, one exit code, no channel telling \"who\" failed → merge.\n- raw file = the only evidence. Exists → something was collected → merge is possible and meaningful (partial data). Doesn't exist → nothing to convert → fail early.\n- Report from a dirty run is diagnostic, not comparable: uninstrumented processes appear as uncovered.\n- If we wanted to distinguish: junit / logs, not exit code.\n\nKeep brief (user asked \"кратко\"). Tags.\n\nLet me be careful about \"instrumentation\" terminology: dynamic instrumentation happens at process start; data is collected during execution; the .coverage is written at session end. If one process fails to instrument, collect may log it and continue (observed? not proven). Mark as assumption.\n\nAnswer.",
