log-level` \"Log level. Allowed values: Error, Info, Verbose.\" I'd guess the default level is Info or Error. If default is Error and nothing errored, would it create an empty file? Probably it would create the file anyway.\n\nHmm. Since no file exists, the most likely explanation is that logging is off unless `-ll` is given.\n\nTest idea (read-only? no — running collect writes files). Can't in plan mode. But next execution step: add `-ll Info` and see if the file appears. That's a cheap experiment (could run a narrow collect like before, ~10 s).\n\nAlternatively: drop `-l` entirely — do we actually need the log? Our purpose was to distinguish \"test failure vs collector failure\". We have ctest output and the raw file. The log is a nice-to-have. If it doesn't work without `-ll`, adding `-ll Info` is trivial.\n\nLet me read cv_debug.log for clues.\n\nAlso: maybe the tool refuses to overwrite/creates only if parent dir exists — reports exists.\n\nLet me read the temp file.\n\n5. Also worth checking: `LastTest.log` is 856 KB (contains all 587 tests) — fine.\n\n6. The stale `LastTestsFailed.log`: I should report it as a real staleness trap. Actually, does ctest remove it? Let me check the CMake docs... I believe CTest writes `LastTestsFailed.log` only when there are failures and does NOT delete it on success. Hmm, I recall it being deleted. Empirically here: not deleted. So note it.\n\nWait — maybe because the run was under `collect` and ctest's `--output-on-failure`... no, unrelated. Or because the green run's ctest invocation has `-E` exclusions and ... no.\n\nLet me verify with a quick look at the LastTest.log end: does it say \"End testing\" and no failures? Yes 100% passed.\n\nSo stale file confirmed.\n\nLet me read cv_debug.log.",
