ed — hmm, the full number is *lower* than t1_c alone (41%) because unit-test modules dilute. That's expected under the per-copy metric, and it's exactly why the union matters. So \"content check\" must use union (96.4%) not root. Worth stating.\n- Step 5.3: decided — union needed.\n- Step 6: analysis of uncovered lines (union shows 25 uncovered lines):\n  - string_utils.c: 2 uncovered (82 valid, 80 covered)\n  - t3_bbox_c main.c: 11 uncovered (82/71)\n  - t3_bbox_cpp main.cpp: 2 (49/47)\n  - t4_filter_c main.c: 8 (83/75)\n  - t4_filter_cpp main.cpp: 2 (61/59)\n  - everything else 100%\n  \n  That's a good concrete basis for step 6. I can identify the uncovered lines per file from the union map. Might be useful to report now? The user asked \"check the green run\", but the next step is analysis. I'll summarize the uncovered counts and list line numbers for common (string_utils.c 2 lines) — that's the shared code and most interesting. Let me compute which lines are uncovered in the union, per file. That's read-only and directly useful.\n\nLet me compute uncovered line numbers for each file (union). Then the answer can include a short table.\n\nThis is step 6 data. Let me get it.\n\nNote: the union analysis uses cobertura's hits. For lines that are \"partial\" in native, cobertura says hits>0 → covered. Fine.\n\nLet me compute the uncovered line numbers.\n\nAlso: should I report the `main.c` duplicates issue in the union table (two files named main.c → I printed by leaf name, causing ambiguity: two `main.c` rows). I should map full paths to tasks for clarity.\n\nLet me produce a per-file table with task folder names and uncovered line numbers.",
