          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Two questions:\n\n1. Do I recommend making coverage.log appear — is there a point?\n2. LastTestsFailed.log — was it also stale on normal runs? i.e., always? Can it be changed or only documented as a known limitation?\n\nFor #2, I need to check whether ctest deletes LastTestsFailed.log on success. Empirically: on the green run it wasn't deleted (mtime 12:07:55). But do we have evidence from earlier normal runs? Let's look: are there other build dirs with LastTestsFailed.log? Check `build/debug/Testing/Temporary/LastTestsFailed.log` mtime vs `LastTest.log` mtime — if debug has both, and LastTestsFailed is older than LastTest, that indicates staleness across green runs. Let me inspect.\n\nAlso verify ctest behavior/documentation. I can check `ctest --help` for `--output-junit` etc.; docs about LastTestsFailed? Not in help. Could search CMake docs locally? Maybe not. The authoritative: CTest writes `LastTestsFailed.log` when tests fail; does it remove on success? I believe CTest only writes it if there are failures and does NOT remove it otherwise. Actually, I recall `LastTestsFailed.log` is written by CTest at the end of a run *if* tests failed; there's no deletion. Let me verify empirically with the debug build: if debug's LastTestsFailed.log exists with an old timestamp while LastTest.log is recent and green, that proves it persists.\n\nBut wait — has the debug build ever had a failing test? The user has run negative controls many times in debug (journal says). So likely yes.\n\nLet me inspect timestamps in build/debug and build/release, plus contents.\n\nAlso, is there a way to make our harness handle it? Options:\n- Delete `LastTestsFailed.log` before running ctest inside collect? We can't easily: ctest is a child of collect, and the script could delete the file before invoking collect. That's a one-line `file(REMOVE ...)` of ctest's temp file. But it's touching CMake's own build tree state; acceptable? It would make stale info impossible. However, if the red run fails, ctest writes a fresh one — good. So deleting before each run is a legit fix, if we care.\n- Or in `coverage.cmake`, remove `${BUILD_DIR}/Testing/Temporary/LastTestsFailed.log` at start. That guarantees it reflects the current run.\n- Or document only.\n- Better: use ctest's own flag? There's `--output-junit <file>` which we control; that's the machine-readable, fresh-by-construction alternative. But it's an extra artifact. Hmm.\n\nIs the stale file actually harmful? For a human debugging: yes, potentially. For scripts: our harness doesn't use it. The course's §4.10 says avoid traps; but this is CTest's internal state, not ours.\n\nI should also check: does CTest delete `LastTestsFailed.log` when a run has no failures? Let me look at CMake source knowledge: In `cmCTest::RunTests`, at the end, `if (failed) { ... WriteLastTestsFailedLog }`. I don't recall a remove. I think it's only written, never removed. Let me verify empirically from the repo's build dirs.\n\nLet me inspect:\n- build/debug/Testing/Temporary/LastTestsFailed.log (exists? mtime? content?)\n- build/debug/Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log mtime\n- build/release similarly.\n\nIf debug's LastTestsFailed is old relative to LastTest, and the last debug run was green, it proves persistence. Also content might list an old test.\n\nRegarding #1 (coverage.log): recommendation. What's the actual value?\n- On a red run, we already have ctest output and LastTest.log with per-test output.\n- The collector log could contain instrumentation diagnostics (which processes failed to instrument, skipped modules). But `coverage.settings.xml` explicitly filters modules, and `coverage.xml` (native) has `<skipped_module>`/`<skipped_function>` lists — that's the *authoritative* record of what was skipped, not the log! So the log's incremental value is low.\n- Also, the log adds a file that must be cleaned, and the `-ll` level adds noise.\n- Conclusion [МНЕНИЕ]: not worth it; drop `-l`. If later a real need appears (e.g., diagnosing partial instrumentation), the native XML's skipped lists are better. Recommend removing `-l` rather than adding `-ll`.\n- But one caveat: does the log contain a per-session summary of instrumentation failures that skipped_* doesn't? Unknown. We can test cheaply by adding `-ll Info` once and looking. But is it worth 10 s + a file? Could do it as a diagnostic experiment and then decide. That's a reasonable \"probe\" in the user's style: run once, inspect, decide.\n\nWait — plan mode forbids any command that modifies files, including running collect (it writes). So I can't test now. I can propose it as an optional experiment.\n\nAlso relevant: the user removed `file(MAKE_DIRECTORY logs)`; the `-l` path points into reports/. Fine.\n\nAnswer structure:\n1. coverage.log: [МНЕНИЕ] не нужен. Reasons: ctest output + LastTest.log + native xml's skipped_module/skipped_function cover the need; `-l` adds a file to clean and a log-level knob; the tool silently not creating it is itself a signal that it's not a supported/needed path. Recommendation: remove `-l` (and don't add coverage.log to REMOVE). If curiosity: one 10-second probe with `-ll Info` to see whether it produces anything; but decide by value, not by \"it's there\".\n   Also note: our earlier justification (distinguish test failure vs collector failure) isn't actually satisfied by the log either — the log would be written by the collector, and we can't know the tool's semantics. The real distinguishing evidence is: raw exists + ctest output.\n\n2. LastTestsFailed.log: explain what it is (CTest's own file in the build tree, not ours), and whether it persists. Need evidence. Let me check.\n\nLet me inspect the build dirs.\n\nAlso: can we change it? Options:\n a) `file(REMOVE \"${BUILD_DIR}/Testing/Temporary/LastTestsFailed.log\")` at the start of coverage.cmake — only affects coverage runs, not normal `ctest --preset full`.\n b) For normal runs, there's nothing built-in; you'd need a wrapper (not worth it). Hmm, is there a ctest option to clear? No. There's `--rerun-failed` which *reads* it — that's exactly why CTest keeps it! `ctest --rerun-failed` uses LastTestsFailed.log. So it's by design: the last failing set is preserved so you can rerun. That reframes the \"stale\" finding: it's a feature, not a bug. It's not meant to say \"tests currently failing\"; it's the last non-empty failure set.\n \n That's an important insight: `ctest --rerun-failed` depends on it. So removing it would break `--rerun-failed`. And normal ctest runs leave it, by design, so that after a green run you can still rerun the last failures? Hmm, that seems odd but plausible.\n\n Let me verify with `ctest --help` for `--rerun-failed` description: \"Run tests that failed in the previous test run\" — reads LastTestsFailed.log. So yes, enabling that behavior is intentional. But does CTest clear it on success? Let me check empirically.\n\nActually, I recall CMake's implementation: `cmCTest::RunTests` calls `this->MarkTestsFailed()`... At the end: if there are failed tests, write LastTestsFailed.log; **else remove the file**? Hmm. Let me just check the filesystem evidence across debug/release.\n\nLet me run the checks.",
