          "text": "Excellent evidence:\n\n- `build/debug`: LastTest.log from 21.10 18:46 (a green run — the journal ends with green runs on 21.10), while LastTestsFailed.log is from **20.10 20:01** (from the red negative-control run of t4). So the debug build had a green run (21.10) after a red run (20.10), and the LastTestsFailed.log was **not removed**. This proves persistence across normal (non-coverage) runs too. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n- `build/release`: LastTestsFailed.log absent, LastTest.log 21.10 18:47. So in release it never had a failure → file never created. Confirms: created on failure, never removed on success.\n- `build/coverage`: the same pattern.\n\nSo: CTest writes it when there are failures and does not delete it on a later green run. It's by design because `ctest --rerun-failed` reads it (\"Run only the tests that failed previously\"). Actually, the help says \"failed previously\" — so preserving it is intentional: you can rerun the last failing set even after a green run? That seems odd, but the file's purpose is exactly that.\n\nHmm, is it truly never removed? Let me be careful: maybe CTest removes it on a green run only when the previous run also had no failures? No — the evidence: debug green at 18:46 on 21.10, file from 20.10 survived. Strong.\n\nWait: was the 21.10 18:46 run green? The journal's last entries: 2026-10-21 \"прогоны... 100% tests passed\" — yes, they ran ctest --preset full and full-release on 21.10. The debug LastTest.log at 18:46 on 21.10. So green. And the failed file from 20.10 (the negative control on t4, which the journal describes: t4 negative tests at 20.10). So yes.\n\nConclusion: not a defect of our harness, and not something to \"fix\" — it's ctest semantics tied to `--rerun-failed`. Recommendation: leave as is, maybe one line in journal/README noting that `LastTestsFailed.log` is \"last set of failures, not current state\", so nobody is misled. Not a \"known limitation\" of the project, more a note about tooling. I'd put it in the journal only (it's not a project limitation; it's standard CTest behavior). Or in README troubleshooting? Probably journal.\n\nCan we change it? Yes, by deleting the file at the start of a run — but that breaks `--rerun-failed` for the coverage flow and doesn't affect normal flows. Not worth it. Document only.\n\nNow #1 coverage.log:\n\nEvidence from native XML: `<skipped_module>`/`<skipped_function>` lists — we already saw 49/38 in t1. These are the authoritative record of what wasn't instrumented. So the log's value is unclear.\n\nAlso: the collector log might record per-session warnings. But we can't verify without enabling `-ll` (needs a run; plan mode forbids).\n\nRecommendation: remove `-l` entirely. Reasons:\n- its purpose (distinguish failure classes) isn't served: we already have ctest output + LastTestsFailed + the existence of raw.\n- instrumentation gaps are visible in native XML (skipped_*).\n- an option that silently does nothing is worse than no option: it suggests a log exists.\n- one less artifact to clean.\n\nIf the user wants to know what `-ll` would give, a 10-second probe is possible later — but decide by need, not curiosity. Actually, curiosity is legitimate in the user's practice (\"sandbox probes\"). I can offer: probe once with `-ll Info` into sandbox, inspect, then decide. But it writes files (build or sandbox) — allowed in execution, not now.\n\nCareful: is my claim \"skipped_* is authoritative\" right? `<skipped_module>` lists modules not instrumented; `<skipped_function>` lists functions. Yes. And in the full run, we should check that product modules are not in skipped. Earlier for t1: skipped included gtest_main.dll, t1_e2e.exe (excluded by ModulePaths intentionally). For the full run we should check `common_c_shared.dll` isn't skipped — it wasn't (it appears as a module with block metrics). Good.\n\nSo the answer:\n\n1. No, don't chase coverage.log. Evidence: the native XML already lists skipped modules/functions; ctest output gives test failures. Recommend removing `-l`. If you want to verify that `-ll` would produce something, one probe; but I see no consumer.\n\n2. LastTestsFailed.log is CTest's by design: written only when there are failures, never deleted on a green run. Evidence: debug build — LastTest.log 21.10 18:46 (green), LastTestsFailed.log 20.10 20:01 (old negative control). Release has none because it never failed. And `ctest --rerun-failed` depends on it. So it's not our defect and not worth \"fixing\"; document semantics (one sentence in journal/README) so nobody reads it as \"current state\".\n\nShould we add it to README \"known limitations\"? It's not a project limitation — it's CTest behavior. I'd put it in the journal + maybe a note in README's coverage section? I'd say journal only. But the user asked \"или только в изв.ограничения?\" — answer: neither, it's a tool semantic; a one-line note in the journal is enough. If they want discoverability, a line in README troubleshooting.\n\nHmm, but is there a subtle risk for *our* harness? Could a future script read LastTestsFailed? No. So no.\n\nLet me also add: if we ever need \"which tests failed in THIS run\" machine-readably, use `ctest --output-junit` — fresh by construction. That's the cleaner answer than deleting LastTestsFailed.\n\nNow write the answer concisely with tags and the evidence.\n\nAlso plan mode: no edits. End with recommendation (remove -l; document LastTestsFailed semantics in journal).\n\nLength moderate.",
