          "text": "The user asks a simple conceptual question: why can't branches ending in `exit(io_fail)` be tested by an in-process test? Answer: yes, because `exit()` terminates the process immediately — the test process dies, gtest can't report, no cleanup, no assertions after. It's testable only with a process-level test (spawn the exe and check exit code/stderr) or death tests (gtest's `EXPECT_EXIT` / death test which forks/spawns a subprocess).\n\nBut note: for the *coverage* stage, the e2e tests are exactly process-level, so exiting branches could be covered if you can provoke OOM — but you can't provoke OOM. So the two obstacles combine: exit requires process-level test (we have those), and OOM requires injection (we don't have).\n\nLet me be precise:\n- `exit()` in the tested code: an in-process unit test would die. gtest death tests (`EXPECT_EXIT`) run the statement in a child process, so they *can* test exit paths — but they're a gtest feature (DeathTest). So \"нельзя внутрипроцессным тестом\" is accurate; death test is a subprocess mechanism.\n- However, for the coverage metric, e2e already covers code in a child process; so if OOM could be injected, the e2e test would cover the branch and the exit code would be observable. The blocker is injection, not exit per se.\n- Also: exit inside a helper skips destructors/flush? `exit` flushes stdio and runs atexit; it doesn't unwind the stack. For C++ code, `exit` skips destructors — design smell if used in C++.\n\nSo answer briefly: yes, because exit terminates the whole process; in-process test can't continue/assert. Options: death test (subprocess), e2e (subprocess) — but both still need to provoke OOM.\n\nKeep it short (user asked \"ответь кратко\").\n\nAlso note: this is C code (t3_c/t4_c), so exit is normal-ish. For C++ I'd avoid.\n\nAnswer 4-6 sentences.",
