          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user responds to my unverified list:\n1. Union is computed by an ad-hoc script — they say the summary tool will come later; if there's a discrepancy, they'll fix it. So they're accepting the risk.\n2. Release build just done: 593/593 passed, format-check included. So that's verified (by them). I should acknowledge and maybe ask for the exact output/artifact? They stated it. I can accept but note it's per their statement (I didn't see the log). Should I ask for a log file? That's the discipline: status \"проверено\" requires command+output. They gave the output line. Fine.\n3. Do I see sense in verifying `--no-tests=error` through the target? \n4. Do I see sense in an experiment for \"partial instrumentation is not detected\"?\n\nThey want my opinion on 3 and 4.\n\nLet me think:\n\n**--no-tests=error through the target**: The target hardcodes ctest without a filter. To test the end-to-end behavior, you'd need a condition where ctest finds no tests. With the current command (`--test-dir build/coverage --output-on-failure --no-tests=error -E \"format-check|e2e/runner/\"`), tests always exist. The only way to trigger \"no tests\" through the target is to break the build/test registration massively (e.g., delete CTestTestfile) — artificial. So the meaningful aspects are:\n- the flag is present in the command (verified by reading);\n- ctest's behavior with the flag (verified: exit 8 in journal);\n- that collect propagates the non-zero (verified: rc=8).\nWhat remains is just \"the script sees rc!=0 and follows the report-then-fail path\" — which was already verified with a real red test. So an end-to-end no-tests test adds nothing new and requires an artificial break. Recommendation: **no**, don't do it. The property is covered by composition of already-verified facts.\n\nAlternatively, could the flag be wrong (typo) and silently ignored? ctest would fail on an unknown option, so a typo would break every run (seen). Since runs work, the flag is accepted. Is it possible that `--no-tests=error` is accepted but the \"no tests\" situation never arises? That's the point of the flag. So no further value.\n\n**Partial instrumentation experiment**: What would it mean? Deliberately make one module uninstrumentable and see: (a) does collect return 0? (b) is the result visible as skipped_module? (c) does the script report anything? This is a real experiment. But how to make a module partial? Options:\n- Add a module path to the settings Exclude? That's normal filtering, not \"failure\".\n- Remove a PDB for a target (e.g., delete `t3_bbox_c/main.pdb`) before collect? Then the dynamic instrumentation may fail to map lines for that module. But dynamic instrumentation uses PDBs at runtime; if missing, the module may be skipped and logged. That's a plausible experiment.\n- Make a target without debug info (strip /Zi) — but coverage flags add /Zi globally.\n- Corrupt a PDB.\nThis is doable but the outcome is uncertain and may produce a misleading \"success\" (e.g., tool logs a warning but rc=0, report lacks the module). The value: confirms that the harness doesn't silently produce a partial report without failing. That's an important property if we rely on coverage for decisions. But the course stage's deliverable is the analysis; a partial instrumentation scenario is unlikely in normal operation. And the experiment could be fiddly.\n\nMy recommendation: **not now**; it's a \"known unknown\" to note. If we ever see `skipped_module` containing a product module, that's the signal. Actually, wait — we already see `skipped_module` entries for non-product modules. A relevant check that's cheap: in the current full report, verify that **no product module** (t*/main.exe, common_*_shared.dll, tests/unit/*.exe) appears in `skipped_module`/`skipped_function`. That's a read-only check of the existing artifact and directly answers \"did we silently lose instrumentation?\" much more valuable than an artificial experiment. Let me propose that instead. I can even do it now (read-only) — plan mode allows reading. Let me check the native XML for skipped modules/functions and whether any product file appears.\n\nThat's a great substitute: instead of an artificial experiment, verify the *actual* report has no product skips. If it does, we have a real problem. Let me run it now (read-only) to give a concrete answer.\n\nLet me inspect `build/coverage/reports/coverage.xml` for skipped modules/functions. The native XML has `<skipped_modules>` with `<skipped_module name=... path=...>` and `<skipped_functions>` maybe with reasons. Let me check for product names.\n\nAlso check whether `skipped_function` includes functions from product files (e.g., inline functions?). If product functions are skipped, that could hide coverage.\n\nLet me run:\n- list skipped_module names, filter t[1-4]_, common_, tests/unit.\n- list skipped_function entries with their module/source? Need to see structure.\n- count.\n\nThis directly addresses #4 with evidence, without an experiment.\n\nAlso for #3, I can state no need and why.\n\nLet me run the check.\n\nAlso: the user said release 593 with format-check. I should acknowledge and update the status to \"проверено (по твоему выводу)\". Also note: if they want a record, the log would be good; but accept.\n\nLet me inspect skipped entries.",
